00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:14.601
- [Voiceover] Christopher
asks, "What are your thoughts

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.732
"on employing friends?"

00:01:17.597 --> 00:01:19.733
- Christoper, this is a great question

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.763
for a lot of people who watch my stuff.

00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:24.981
They make that connection on
the family business thing.

00:01:24.981 --> 00:01:27.953
I get enormous amounts
of, excuse me, email

00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:30.739
from people that are
the sons and daughters

00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:32.875
of business owners because
they knew my narrative

00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:35.288
now that I'm running Vayner with AJ,

00:01:35.288 --> 00:01:36.949
I'm getting some more
brother and sister stuff,

00:01:36.949 --> 00:01:38.135
and I'm getting a little older.

00:01:38.135 --> 00:01:39.377
I'm even getting some of the perspective

00:01:39.377 --> 00:01:41.514
from the dads and the moms.

00:01:43.214 --> 00:01:45.055
I have employed friends at both

00:01:45.055 --> 00:01:47.760
Wine Library and VaynerMedia.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.743
VaynerMedia was started with
five of AJ's dear friends

00:01:51.743 --> 00:01:53.876
that I think the number
one advice I'd say is

00:01:53.876 --> 00:01:55.676
you should absolutely.

00:01:55.676 --> 00:01:58.253
I'm emphatic about this, and you guys know

00:01:58.253 --> 00:02:00.726
I'm big on, you know,
do what works for you,

00:02:00.726 --> 00:02:04.174
but I'm a huge fan of hiring friends,

00:02:04.174 --> 00:02:06.067
especially early on, to establish culture,

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.140
to have those teammates in the trenches,

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.499
especially if you're willing
to practice meritocracy.

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:16.074
So the key to friends is that,
here we are five years later,

00:02:16.074 --> 00:02:18.663
and AJ's five to six high
school and college friends

00:02:18.663 --> 00:02:20.184
that started the company with us

00:02:20.184 --> 00:02:22.437
are in different places
within the organization

00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:25.711
and not really treated any
differently at the highest levels

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:29.368
than anybody else, and very honestly,

00:02:29.368 --> 00:02:32.154
I hope they're not listening
or watching the show,

00:02:32.154 --> 00:02:36.554
they may even be treated
slightly harder and worse

00:02:36.554 --> 00:02:39.503
than everybody else because
I'm so sensitive to it

00:02:39.503 --> 00:02:40.989
in the other direction.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:43.242
I expect the six of you not
to hit me up for a raise.

00:02:44.932 --> 00:02:46.902
I think that I'm a big fan of it.

00:02:46.902 --> 00:02:48.297
I recommend it.

00:02:48.297 --> 00:02:50.526
It is risky.

00:02:50.526 --> 00:02:52.661
I have fired my friends in the past.

00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:55.333
Brandon was my best friend growing up.

00:02:55.333 --> 00:02:57.097
He runs Wine Library.

00:02:57.097 --> 00:02:58.424
Risky.

00:02:59.858 --> 00:03:03.387
You know, it really comes
down to, here's my belief.

00:03:03.387 --> 00:03:05.126
My belief is very simple.

00:03:05.126 --> 00:03:09.456
If you loose a friendship
from somebody working for you

00:03:09.456 --> 00:03:11.430
and not working out and
you having to fire them

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.598
or them leaving, then your friendship

00:03:13.598 --> 00:03:16.718
wasn't as strong as you thought.

00:03:16.718 --> 00:03:19.989
That's the bottom line.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:20.570
Stone...

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.021
(all chuckling)

00:03:22.021 --> 00:03:23.867
I didn't do it, I didn't do it.

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:26.503
- [Voiceover] Columbia asks,
"Any thoughts on bringing

00:03:26.503 --> 00:03:29.530
"relevance to the funeral
business industry?"

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.424
- Columbia, great question.

00:03:30.424 --> 00:03:31.085
This was a fun one.

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:33.081
India, you're dark.

00:03:33.081 --> 00:03:36.242
Because India was like,
pumped for this question.

00:03:36.242 --> 00:03:38.800
I assume you're talking
the funeral home business.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.308
Ironically, I've addressed
this in other places,

00:03:41.308 --> 00:03:44.596
either in a blog post or a public speech.

00:03:44.596 --> 00:03:46.273
This is an industry I'm fascinated by.

00:03:46.273 --> 00:03:48.723
I once gave the recommendation to somebody

00:03:48.723 --> 00:03:50.151
in the funeral business to become

00:03:50.151 --> 00:03:54.005
the number one flower
content site on the Internet,

00:03:54.005 --> 00:03:56.002
because that was the connection
to something positive

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.975
that I could make, and it was
a really really interesting,

00:03:58.975 --> 00:04:01.053
you know, back to
everybody's a media company,

00:04:01.053 --> 00:04:02.608
you guys have heard my spiel on this.

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.896
If you haven't, Google it around.

00:04:04.896 --> 00:04:07.717
I think companies should
become media companies

00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:12.512
and play in sections that are
related to their industry.

00:04:12.512 --> 00:04:14.230
If you're a funeral
home, you can't start a

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.960
reviewing other funeral homes business,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.805
you have vested interest,
but something like flowers

00:04:17.805 --> 00:04:20.755
is an interesting
gateway to that business.

00:04:20.755 --> 00:04:21.810
You know, look.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.110
I'm gonna use your question as it ended,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.871
which is innovation is what awakes

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:28.763
sleepy gray-haired industries.

00:04:28.763 --> 00:04:30.304
I came along in the wine world.

00:04:30.304 --> 00:04:33.090
That was a very pretentious, very this,

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.517
very look down on, very gray haired.

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:38.025
Wine experts were 60 year old dudes.

00:04:38.025 --> 00:04:41.891
They definitely weren't 25
year old Jet fans from Jersey,

00:04:41.891 --> 00:04:43.806
and so I came in there and I innovated.

00:04:43.806 --> 00:04:47.090
First it was launching
a dot com site in 1996.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:48.741
Later it was doing a YouTube show

00:04:48.741 --> 00:04:50.134
less than a year after YouTube came out

00:04:50.134 --> 00:04:51.771
around the subject.

00:04:51.771 --> 00:04:55.115
The way you wake up an industry

00:04:55.115 --> 00:04:56.833
is by innovating.

00:04:56.833 --> 00:04:58.493
Looking at things like Snapchat,

00:04:58.493 --> 00:05:00.757
looking at things like smart technology

00:05:00.757 --> 00:05:03.718
and augmented reality and Pinterest

00:05:03.718 --> 00:05:06.376
and things of that nature, and so

00:05:06.376 --> 00:05:09.964
obviously, it sounds like you're
in a very sensitive space.

00:05:09.964 --> 00:05:12.123
You've got to be careful.

00:05:12.123 --> 00:05:16.199
Obviously death is at the height
of emotion in our society.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.418
One thing I've been spending
a lot of weird zen time on

00:05:20.418 --> 00:05:23.147
is we don't know how to
die gracefully in America.

00:05:23.147 --> 00:05:25.283
I'm very fascinated by that, meaning

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:28.186
we fight and like medicine
ourselves up so intensely.

00:05:28.186 --> 00:05:29.695
I'm getting on a little bit of a tangent,

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.772
but I get it, because boy, out
of all the passions I have,

00:05:32.772 --> 00:05:34.780
the number one core passion I have

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.417
is I do not want to die,

00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:38.867
and it is not even a
remotely close second.

00:05:38.867 --> 00:05:40.539
Fuck the Jets and everything else.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:43.708
That is my number one passion.

00:05:43.708 --> 00:05:46.332
So I get it, so I would
say navigate carefully

00:05:46.332 --> 00:05:48.352
based on the question, but
to go on a higher level

00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:51.229
of waking up a gray haired industry,

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:54.354
disrespect the gray haired industry.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.302
Understand your history, understand it,

00:05:57.302 --> 00:06:00.101
but don't respect it to a fault.

00:06:00.101 --> 00:06:00.960
Get it?

00:06:01.569 --> 00:06:03.915
- [Voiceover] Kobus asks, "What
would be your top three tips

00:06:03.915 --> 00:06:06.399
"for switching on your brain?"

00:06:07.623 --> 00:06:09.598
- Ko, this is an
interesting question to me.

00:06:09.598 --> 00:06:10.213
Like that?

00:06:10.213 --> 00:06:12.783
Ko, this is an interesting question to me.

00:06:13.649 --> 00:06:15.111
I don't really know how to answer it.

00:06:15.111 --> 00:06:18.569
First of all, I don't do well
with top three questions,

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:21.242
so VaynerNation, don't
ask me top three questions

00:06:21.242 --> 00:06:23.238
because I don't even know
how to gather my thoughts

00:06:23.238 --> 00:06:25.096
in that way, I'm not an
active enough thinker

00:06:25.096 --> 00:06:26.840
to execute that.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.168
I don't know.

00:06:29.168 --> 00:06:31.467
You know, weirdly the only
thing that comes to me

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:33.057
on the answer, and it's
why I took this question,

00:06:33.057 --> 00:06:35.182
because it's an interesting question,

00:06:35.182 --> 00:06:37.237
is passion.

00:06:37.237 --> 00:06:39.083
It's a very lightweight answer.

00:06:39.083 --> 00:06:42.101
It's a fluffy answer, but I truly believe

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:44.271
that it is the answer, meaning,

00:06:44.271 --> 00:06:46.873
if you actually love what you're doing,

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:49.578
if you actually love
it, there is no friction

00:06:49.578 --> 00:06:50.519
to turn on your brain.

00:06:50.519 --> 00:06:52.690
The only time I feel like
I have to turn on my brain

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.823
was when I was six to 22 years old,

00:06:56.823 --> 00:06:59.006
when I was going through the
bullshit education system

00:06:59.006 --> 00:07:00.364
of America, right?

00:07:00.364 --> 00:07:02.071
That's when I felt like
I had to turn it on,

00:07:02.071 --> 00:07:06.318
to appease horse crap that
didn't match my reality.

00:07:06.318 --> 00:07:08.793
But every since that
day, when like, "Yay!"

00:07:08.793 --> 00:07:11.382
and I went into like, you
need to buy this Pinot Grigio,

00:07:11.382 --> 00:07:14.133
the second that started in May of 1998,

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.224
there's never been a day
that I've had to activate.

00:07:16.224 --> 00:07:18.161
It's always on, and I don't mean

00:07:18.161 --> 00:07:20.355
always on buzzword marketing,

00:07:20.355 --> 00:07:22.559
it's that I love what I do so much

00:07:22.559 --> 00:07:24.614
that there is no friction to turn it on,

00:07:24.614 --> 00:07:27.435
even when I am landing
at 2:00 in the morning

00:07:27.435 --> 00:07:29.478
from a flight that's delayed
like the other night,

00:07:29.478 --> 00:07:32.195
and then going directly into
it at 6:30 in the morning

00:07:32.195 --> 00:07:34.285
because the fire is so deep inside,

00:07:34.285 --> 00:07:35.968
you love it so much.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:37.978
You don't need that jump start.

00:07:38.554 --> 00:07:40.609
- [Voiceover] Cory asks,
"When it comes to weaknesses

00:07:40.609 --> 00:07:42.455
"within yourself, how do you determine

00:07:42.455 --> 00:07:44.733
"whether to delegate or to strengthen it?"

00:07:45.668 --> 00:07:47.966
- Cory, I love this question

00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:50.412
because I really don't
know the full answer

00:07:50.412 --> 00:07:51.609
and I'm not sure anybody does.

00:07:51.609 --> 00:07:52.712
I think that's a question that we all have

00:07:52.712 --> 00:07:54.208
to answer for ourselves.

00:07:54.208 --> 00:07:56.439
You know, I always talk
about betting on strengths,

00:07:56.439 --> 00:07:58.842
but there's clearly been
weaknesses that I've created

00:07:58.842 --> 00:08:01.954
at least a nice baseline, a foundation.

00:08:01.954 --> 00:08:03.022
As a matter of fact, one of the weaknesses

00:08:03.022 --> 00:08:04.868
I've been working on
for the last 36 months,

00:08:04.868 --> 00:08:08.026
especially in building Vayner,
and I give AJ, my brother,

00:08:08.026 --> 00:08:10.289
a lot of credit for this,
is leading with a little bit

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:13.761
more reality than
over-honeying the situation.

00:08:13.761 --> 00:08:16.106
I'm such a positive
dude that a lot of times

00:08:16.106 --> 00:08:18.637
I don't think I was clear
enough with negative feedback

00:08:18.637 --> 00:08:21.934
or critical feedback to
an employee or a teammate

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.862
because I was hedging too much,

00:08:23.862 --> 00:08:25.046
I was like, "You're the best!

00:08:25.046 --> 00:08:25.789
"This is all great!

00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:26.485
"Don't worry!

00:08:26.485 --> 00:08:27.797
"But maybe you should..."

00:08:27.797 --> 00:08:29.817
You know, I've been a
little bit more direct,

00:08:29.817 --> 00:08:32.813
and that's a weakness
that I've strengthened,

00:08:32.813 --> 00:08:35.642
no question about it, because
I thought it was important,

00:08:35.642 --> 00:08:37.344
and because I felt like
it was a couple inches off

00:08:37.344 --> 00:08:38.924
and just by moving it a little bit.

00:08:38.924 --> 00:08:40.328
Does that mean that I'm, like,

00:08:40.328 --> 00:08:42.268
the scary guy in the building?

00:08:42.268 --> 00:08:44.404
I mean, I'm like the least
scary guy in the building

00:08:44.404 --> 00:08:46.692
outside of the reputation of who I am

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.886
or being the CEO of a company,

00:08:48.886 --> 00:08:50.558
but once people get to
know me a little bit,

00:08:50.558 --> 00:08:54.308
like, I'm the pushover that
way because I am positive,

00:08:54.308 --> 00:08:56.665
but I think that, look.

00:08:56.665 --> 00:08:59.878
I would say this is an
80 20 rule answer to me.

00:08:59.878 --> 00:09:02.630
I truly still believe,
minimumly, that you need to spend

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.977
80% of your time on your strengths,

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:07.185
and if you want to
allocate 20% of your time

00:09:07.185 --> 00:09:09.143
to audit, you know, I would actually

00:09:09.143 --> 00:09:10.756
just do a whole lot of listening.

00:09:10.756 --> 00:09:12.359
I would actually ask the
people you work with,

00:09:12.359 --> 00:09:13.763
the people that work for you,

00:09:13.763 --> 00:09:17.049
the people you work for, your
friends and contemporaries,

00:09:17.049 --> 00:09:18.393
the people that are closest to you,

00:09:18.393 --> 00:09:20.741
the 10 closest people to
you, friends and relatives

00:09:20.741 --> 00:09:22.517
on what they think you could work on.

00:09:22.517 --> 00:09:24.128
They're a mirror to that.

00:09:24.128 --> 00:09:25.048
You may take a step back.

00:09:25.048 --> 00:09:25.965
It might hurt.

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:27.846
You've got to roll with
humility and empathy

00:09:27.846 --> 00:09:29.205
if you want to address this,

00:09:29.205 --> 00:09:31.550
and then you can start
addressing those 20%,

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.011
because a lot of times the
reason we can't address something

00:09:34.011 --> 00:09:35.648
is because we can't see it,

00:09:35.648 --> 00:09:38.945
and the best way to see it is
through other people's eyes,

00:09:38.945 --> 00:09:40.176
and you know, it's funny.

00:09:40.176 --> 00:09:43.009
The best way to see it is to
use your own ears, actually.

00:09:43.009 --> 00:09:45.214
It's kind of interesting,
there's something there.

00:09:45.214 --> 00:09:47.590
India, we need to go long forum on that.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.261
And that's that.

00:09:49.261 --> 00:09:50.828
- Hey Gary, it's Sean McCabe.

00:09:50.828 --> 00:09:53.371
I just wanted to say thanks
so much for doing your show.

00:09:53.371 --> 00:09:54.973
It's been the number
one inspiration for me

00:09:54.973 --> 00:09:57.155
starting my own daily video show

00:09:57.155 --> 00:09:58.549
at shawnwest.tv.

00:09:58.549 --> 00:09:59.698
- I like the hustle.
- My question is

00:09:59.698 --> 00:10:02.125
- hustle something that can be taught,

00:10:02.125 --> 00:10:03.255
because you're probably
the only person I know

00:10:03.255 --> 00:10:05.345
that out-hustles me, and I want to help

00:10:05.345 --> 00:10:07.656
other people get that,
but do you think hustle

00:10:07.656 --> 00:10:11.130
is something you either have or you don't?

00:10:12.448 --> 00:10:15.603
- Man, these are tough, this
is a tough question day, jeez.

00:10:15.603 --> 00:10:20.174
You know, I do think
work ethic is a trait.

00:10:22.214 --> 00:10:24.194
I think hustle is
something you're born with,

00:10:24.194 --> 00:10:28.768
but I do think the hustle
meter is fundamentally effected

00:10:28.768 --> 00:10:30.825
by who you're doing it for.

00:10:30.825 --> 00:10:33.542
So it's really easy to
hustle for yourself.

00:10:33.542 --> 00:10:36.909
So if you're a solopreneur, super easy,

00:10:36.909 --> 00:10:38.825
you will be 100% the highest hustle

00:10:38.825 --> 00:10:41.495
that you are capable of within your DNA.

00:10:41.495 --> 00:10:43.515
When you work for somebody else,

00:10:43.515 --> 00:10:45.106
you know, I think the hustle meter

00:10:45.106 --> 00:10:48.519
is completely predicated
on how inspired you are

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:52.153
and how protected you
feel for that leader,

00:10:52.153 --> 00:10:54.777
for her or for him and the
organization they work for.

00:10:54.777 --> 00:10:56.913
I truly believe that the
people that work for me

00:10:56.913 --> 00:11:01.148
hustle way harder because I
instill trust and protection

00:11:01.148 --> 00:11:03.287
and I set a high standard for it,

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:06.817
and then thus I am able
to get them to a place

00:11:06.817 --> 00:11:09.359
where they do hustle harder
by the context and the culture

00:11:09.359 --> 00:11:12.645
from within, but they do it
selfishly for themselves first

00:11:12.645 --> 00:11:15.338
and then equally, because
they don't want to let me down

00:11:15.338 --> 00:11:17.358
or they want to grow up in a meritocracy

00:11:17.358 --> 00:11:18.749
where they know they can grow,

00:11:18.749 --> 00:11:21.004
but that's a selfish play.

00:11:21.004 --> 00:11:23.790
I think there is a level of hustle

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:26.774
that ultimately is predicated on your DNA,

00:11:26.774 --> 00:11:30.455
and then, and then I think

00:11:30.455 --> 00:11:32.184
that at some level it's the context

00:11:32.184 --> 00:11:34.709
of the game you're playing, right?

00:11:34.709 --> 00:11:38.010
I'm blown away by how many
people on my own team here

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:42.236
hustle harder working
for me than a lot of my

00:11:42.236 --> 00:11:44.697
entrepreneurial friends
working for themselves,

00:11:44.697 --> 00:11:46.462
and that, to me, is fascinating.

00:11:46.462 --> 00:11:49.086
I think that comes from motivation,

00:11:49.086 --> 00:11:52.337
and so the level of motivation
you have from within yourself

00:11:52.337 --> 00:11:53.809
and the level of motivation you get

00:11:53.809 --> 00:11:55.750
from outside sources,
whether it's an individual,

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:59.232
an organization, some other variable,

00:11:59.232 --> 00:12:00.998
you want to stick it to your dad who said

00:12:00.998 --> 00:12:02.402
you were never going to be anything,

00:12:02.402 --> 00:12:04.852
or the other way, you want to fulfill

00:12:04.852 --> 00:12:07.592
what your mom told you
you were capable of,

00:12:07.592 --> 00:12:09.670
the neighborhood you came from,

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:12.759
there's just so many
interesting variables.

00:12:12.759 --> 00:12:15.369
I've been thinking about writing a book

00:12:15.369 --> 00:12:17.101
called "I Wish Everyone Was an Immigrant."

00:12:17.101 --> 00:12:20.305
It's insane, it's insane
how motivated I am

00:12:20.305 --> 00:12:22.267
from coming from zippo,
and I didn't even come

00:12:22.267 --> 00:12:23.614
from as much zippo as my parents did

00:12:23.614 --> 00:12:24.891
when they were first here, but I tasted

00:12:24.891 --> 00:12:27.352
those early days, and
it's a level of taste

00:12:27.352 --> 00:12:28.711
that I know AJ doesn't have.

00:12:28.711 --> 00:12:30.023
Not that he can't be more motivated,

00:12:30.023 --> 00:12:31.715
but that happens to be
one of my motivators

00:12:31.715 --> 00:12:33.736
that is not one of his motivators.

00:12:33.736 --> 00:12:35.305
Maybe one of his motivators is to

00:12:35.305 --> 00:12:36.859
stick it to the world and tell them

00:12:36.859 --> 00:12:38.369
that he's better than his brother.

00:12:38.369 --> 00:12:40.052
It could be a million different things.

00:12:40.052 --> 00:12:41.573
They're all fascinating.

00:12:41.573 --> 00:12:44.404
I think that you are, I think that you

00:12:44.404 --> 00:12:46.625
are born with some level of it,

00:12:46.625 --> 00:12:49.167
but I absolutely think this
is a nature, nurture game

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:52.581
where circumstances, look,
you may get motivated

00:12:52.581 --> 00:12:55.007
by something horrible
that happens in your life

00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:56.575
and I don't want to go dark, but you know,

00:12:56.575 --> 00:12:57.852
we've gone dark in the past.

00:12:57.852 --> 00:12:59.429
You know, like, everybody
in your family dies

00:12:59.429 --> 00:13:01.126
on a family vacation
that you didn't go to.

00:13:01.126 --> 00:13:03.262
You are now motivated in a different way

00:13:03.262 --> 00:13:04.934
or you're crumbled by that.

00:13:04.934 --> 00:13:10.027
So it's your inner strength
and makeup and infrastructure.

00:13:10.027 --> 00:13:13.200
I've been fascinated
by watching my friends

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.383
watch my other friends become billionaires

00:13:15.383 --> 00:13:17.159
over the last five years, and some of them

00:13:17.159 --> 00:13:19.214
have been motivated, and
some of them pushed back.

00:13:19.214 --> 00:13:22.627
It's like being the child
of a very successful person.

00:13:22.627 --> 00:13:25.182
You either try to trump your moms or pops,

00:13:25.182 --> 00:13:28.482
or you go so far away from
it you want to go, like,

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:30.989
fish in the Amazon for
the rest of your life,

00:13:30.989 --> 00:13:33.484
and I'm fascinated by that, and so I think

00:13:33.484 --> 00:13:36.400
that's DNA, and then
there's the circumstances

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:37.436
that wrap around it.

00:13:38.254 --> 00:13:40.181
- [Voiceover] Matthew
asks, "Are you worried

00:13:40.181 --> 00:13:42.143
"you might have created a Steve Jobs-esque

00:13:42.143 --> 00:13:44.988
"reality distortion
field at VaynerMedia?"

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:48.076
- Matthew, first and foremost,

00:13:48.076 --> 00:13:51.199
I'm massively flattered by this question.

00:13:51.199 --> 00:13:52.592
I don't even know how
else to really take it

00:13:52.592 --> 00:13:56.476
other than, you know, my
ego is very happy with you.

00:13:57.851 --> 00:14:00.483
I guess not,

00:14:00.483 --> 00:14:02.817
mainly because,

00:14:02.817 --> 00:14:06.497
and I didn't know Steve,
and I knew a lot of people

00:14:06.497 --> 00:14:08.343
that knew him, but everything's hearsay.

00:14:08.343 --> 00:14:12.241
My intuition tells me
that I'm dramatically more

00:14:12.241 --> 00:14:16.761
a people person and maybe, you know,

00:14:16.761 --> 00:14:18.897
the answer's fully no.

00:14:18.897 --> 00:14:20.822
Do I think that people at VaynerMedia

00:14:20.822 --> 00:14:22.275
drink the VaynerMedia Kool-Aid

00:14:22.275 --> 00:14:25.279
and their friends and relatives tell them

00:14:25.279 --> 00:14:27.047
that they're drinking the
VaynerMedia Kool-Aid?

00:14:27.047 --> 00:14:29.611
I do because they tell me
those stories every day.

00:14:29.611 --> 00:14:32.910
But, you know,

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.244
look, I mean, is there
times where I realize

00:14:35.244 --> 00:14:37.086
that if I was a little
more dark and a little bit

00:14:37.086 --> 00:14:40.441
more of a bad guy that I
could have created a cult

00:14:40.441 --> 00:14:42.044
and it could have been real negative?

00:14:42.044 --> 00:14:43.681
I do.

00:14:43.681 --> 00:14:44.783
But I guess at the end of the day

00:14:44.783 --> 00:14:49.346
the reason I feel really okay
is I know what my intent is,

00:14:49.346 --> 00:14:53.661
and I think what matters is if
you know what your intent is,

00:14:53.661 --> 00:14:55.042
you're not fearful of that.

00:14:55.042 --> 00:14:56.982
If I am creating some distortion

00:14:56.982 --> 00:15:00.431
amongst my employees and my friend base

00:15:00.431 --> 00:15:03.879
that consumes my content, I think

00:15:03.879 --> 00:15:05.552
it's gonna be all for good.

00:15:05.552 --> 00:15:06.828
I mean, that's a very, you know.

00:15:06.828 --> 00:15:09.370
It's not like, "Ha ha ha,
I've got them all tricked

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.122
"and I'm gonna make 51
cents on every dollar,"

00:15:12.122 --> 00:15:14.548
it's, "Holy crap, if I
have them all tricked

00:15:14.548 --> 00:15:16.882
"I'm gonna make 49 cents on every dollar."

00:15:16.882 --> 00:15:17.671
Got it?

00:15:17.671 --> 00:15:19.239
And that's a big difference, man.

00:15:19.239 --> 00:15:20.551
Those two cents?

00:15:20.551 --> 00:15:23.021
That's a big difference.

00:15:23.021 --> 00:15:25.288
That two cents is the whole difference,

00:15:25.288 --> 00:15:27.946
and so the answer is,

00:15:27.946 --> 00:15:32.428
I'm flattered, maybe, I don't think so,

00:15:32.428 --> 00:15:36.479
and if it's happening, I
think everybody's gonna win.

